IMPORT prelude

-- Women's Charter 1961 (Singapore) -- enforcing a maintenance order: the attachment of earnings order made with or after a
-- maintenance order (s 71); who may apply to enforce (s 80); what the court must and may do on a maintenance enforcement
-- application (s 81); the arrears report to a credit bureau (s 83); the seven maintenance enforcement orders (s 91B) and
-- their limits (ss 91L, 91N, 91P); the attachment of earnings rules (ss 91C, 91D, 91K); and the three-year limit on
-- recovering arrears (s 91R).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation "version in force from 17/8/2026", as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/WC1961.txt. Part 9 and s 71 as substituted by the Family Justice Reform Act 2023
-- (Act 18 of 2023) wef 16 January 2025: the old s 71 (enforcement by fine and imprisonment per month of arrears) is gone,
-- and ss 71A-71C are repealed. The deposit's s 71 is now ONLY the attachment of earnings order made with the maintenance
-- order; enforcement on default is Part 9 (ss 76-91T).
--
-- WHY. Added on 11 October 2026 for REQ-0269 (event j071 of the Jun Hao scenario, 15 January 2039: an ex-partner applies to
-- enforce a $600-a-month child maintenance order after three missed payments).
--
-- NOT ENCODED: the MEO's role, referral, information-gathering, conciliation and reports (ss 84-91A); witnesses (s 79);
-- variation of the maintenance order on an enforcement application in "prescribed circumstances" (s 81(1)(b), (e) --
-- the circumstances are for regulations); the extension of payment dates (s 81(10)); the rules on several attachment of
-- earnings orders at once (s 91E(2)), the information powers (s 91F), the duty to notify changes of employment (s 91G),
-- what counts as earnings (s 91H), Government employers (s 91J); the banker's guarantee refund mechanics (s 91L(4)-(6));
-- the consecutive or concurrent running of sentences (s 91Q); access to information (s 91S); contempt (s 91T); appeals and
-- costs (Part 9A); reciprocal enforcement of foreign orders. The attachment-of-debt order (s 91B(1)(c)) is made "in
-- accordance with the Family Justice Rules", which were not read.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules conventions of this row. No pipeline, no
-- coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Women's Charter 1961 -- enforcement of maintenance orders`

§§ `80 -- who may apply to enforce`

-- s 80(1): "Any of the following persons may make an application to the court for enforcement of a maintenance order that
-- the person alleges to have been breached: (a) the person who applied for the maintenance order; (b) a person who is a
-- payee under the maintenance order for the benefit of a child and who is -- (i) a person having custody or care and
-- control of the child; or (ii) subject to subsection (2) -- a trustee of the child; (c) ... a person who is a payee ...
-- nominated by the appointed person [the Minister's appointee under s 69(3)(d)] ...; (d) any other person who has a right
-- under any other written law to enforce the maintenance order."
-- (2): a trustee jointly with others "must obtain the consent of all the other trustees".
DECLARE EnforcementApplicant IS ONE OF
    `the person who applied for the maintenance order`
    `a payee with custody or care and control of the child`
    `a payee who is a trustee of the child`
    `a payee nominated by the Minister's appointee`
    `a person with a right under other written law`
    `anyone else`

GIVEN `the enforcer` IS AN EnforcementApplicant, `a joint trustee lacking the other trustees' consent` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `may apply to enforce the maintenance order` IF
    CONSIDER `the enforcer`
    WHEN `anyone else` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a payee who is a trustee of the child` THEN NOT `a joint trustee lacking the other trustees' consent`
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `81(1), (2), (4), 91B -- the court's orders on an enforcement application`

-- s 81(1): "the court may do one or more of the following on a maintenance enforcement application: (a) if the court is
-- satisfied that the maintenance order in question has been breached -- make one or more maintenance enforcement orders
-- ...; ... (c) dismiss the maintenance enforcement application; (d) make a consent order ...".
-- s 81(4)(b): if the court makes a consent order it may also "make any maintenance enforcement order, except a maintenance
-- enforcement order mentioned in section 91B(1)(b)" (imprisonment).
-- s 91B(1): "each of the following is a maintenance enforcement order: (a) a warrant directing that maintenance arrears be
-- levied in the manner by law provided for levying fines imposed by a Magistrate's Court; (b) an order sentencing the
-- respondent to imprisonment for maintenance arrears as at the time the maintenance enforcement application in question is
-- made; (c) an order for attachment of a debt made in accordance with the Family Justice Rules; (d) an order requiring the
-- respondent to furnish security against any future default in maintenance payments by means of a banker's guarantee;
-- (e) an order requiring the respondent to undergo financial counselling or any other similar or related programme ... if
-- the court considers it in the interests of the parties ... or their children to do so; (f) a community service order
-- requiring the respondent to perform unpaid community service; (g) an attachment of earnings order."
-- s 91B(2): (d), (e) and (f) may be made even though the arrears have since been paid; (e) and (f) even though the
-- respondent is no longer liable to pay. s 91P(3)(a): imprisonment likewise even though the arrears have since been paid.
-- s 91P(4): no imprisonment under s 91B(1)(b) "after the date that the court has made a show-payment order in respect of the
-- same maintenance enforcement application".
-- s 91N(2): no community service order unless "suitable arrangements can be made for the respondent to perform the community
-- service under the supervision of a community service officer".
-- s 76(1): an "attachment of earnings order" is directed to "a person appearing to the court to be a respondent's employer"
-- and paid "out of the earnings falling to be paid to the respondent".
-- There is NO fine on the respondent among the s 91B orders: the pre-2025 s 71 fine per month of arrears was repealed.
-- INFERENCE: an attachment of earnings order needs someone who appears to be the respondent's employer (or pays him a
-- pension: "earnings" in s 76(1) includes pensions); the encoding takes that as one input.
-- INFERENCE: a levy warrant and an attachment of a debt are read as needing arrears still unpaid when the order is made
-- (there is something to levy); imprisonment needs arrears AT THE TIME OF THE APPLICATION (s 91B(1)(b)), paid or not since.
DECLARE EnforcementOrder IS ONE OF
    `a warrant to levy the arrears as for a fine`
    `imprisonment for the arrears`
    `attachment of a debt`
    `a banker's guarantee against future default`
    `financial counselling`
    `community service`
    `an attachment of earnings order`

DECLARE EnforcementCase HAS
    `the court is satisfied the order was breached` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a consent order was made` IS A BOOLEAN
    `arrears owing when the application was made` IS A NUMBER
    `arrears still unpaid` IS A NUMBER
    `a show-payment order was made on an earlier date in this application` IS A BOOLEAN
    `counselling is in the interests of the parties or their children` IS A BOOLEAN
    `suitable community service arrangements can be made` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the respondent has an employer or pension payer` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the application` IS AN EnforcementCase
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `an enforcement order is open at all` IF
    `the application`'s `the court is satisfied the order was breached` OR `the application`'s `a consent order was made`

GIVEN `the order` IS AN EnforcementOrder, `the application` IS AN EnforcementCase
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may make the enforcement order` IF
    `an enforcement order is open at all` `the application` AND
    CONSIDER `the order`
    WHEN `a warrant to levy the arrears as for a fine` THEN `the application`'s `arrears still unpaid` > 0
    WHEN `imprisonment for the arrears` THEN
            `the application`'s `the court is satisfied the order was breached`
        AND (NOT `the application`'s `a consent order was made`)
        AND `the application`'s `arrears owing when the application was made` > 0
        AND (NOT `the application`'s `a show-payment order was made on an earlier date in this application`)
    WHEN `attachment of a debt` THEN `the application`'s `arrears still unpaid` > 0
    WHEN `a banker's guarantee against future default` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `financial counselling` THEN `the application`'s `counselling is in the interests of the parties or their children`
    WHEN `community service` THEN `the application`'s `suitable community service arrangements can be made`
    WHEN `an attachment of earnings order` THEN `the application`'s `the respondent has an employer or pension payer`

GIVEN `the condition` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`one if` `the condition` MEANS IF `the condition` THEN 1 ELSE 0

GIVEN `the application` IS AN EnforcementCase
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of enforcement orders open` `the application` MEANS
        `one if` (`the court may make the enforcement order` `a warrant to levy the arrears as for a fine` `the application`)
    +   `one if` (`the court may make the enforcement order` `imprisonment for the arrears` `the application`)
    +   `one if` (`the court may make the enforcement order` `attachment of a debt` `the application`)
    +   `one if` (`the court may make the enforcement order` `a banker's guarantee against future default` `the application`)
    +   `one if` (`the court may make the enforcement order` `financial counselling` `the application`)
    +   `one if` (`the court may make the enforcement order` `community service` `the application`)
    +   `one if` (`the court may make the enforcement order` `an attachment of earnings order` `the application`)

-- s 81(2): "Where the court is satisfied that the maintenance order in question has been breached and no consent order is
-- made on the application and the application is not dismissed under subsection (1)(c) or struck out under
-- subsection (1)(f), the court must make an order -- (a) declaring the amount of maintenance arrears ...; (b) requiring the
-- respondent to make payment of the maintenance arrears ... in a lump sum or in instalments ...; (c) requiring the
-- respondent to prove any payment ...; and (d) subject to subsection (5), specifying a term of imprisonment for the purposes
-- of section 91O". (7): the court "must not make any order under subsection (2)(d) ... specifying any term of imprisonment
-- if special circumstances exist which render the making of the order inappropriate"; (8): these include "the respondent's
-- age and the respondent's state of health and physical condition".
GIVEN `the application` IS AN EnforcementCase, `dismissed or struck out` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court must declare the arrears and order them paid and proved` IF
        `the application`'s `the court is satisfied the order was breached`
    AND (NOT `the application`'s `a consent order was made`)
    AND (NOT `dismissed or struck out`)

GIVEN `the application` IS AN EnforcementCase, `dismissed or struck out` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `special circumstances make a term inappropriate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court must specify a default term of imprisonment` IF
        `the court must declare the arrears and order them paid and proved` `the application` `dismissed or struck out`
    AND (NOT `special circumstances make a term inappropriate`)

§§ `91P, 81(5) -- the imprisonment caps`

-- s 91P(1): the sentence under s 91B(1)(b) "must not exceed -- (a) one month for each month's worth of maintenance arrears
-- (or any part thereof); or (b) in a case where the maintenance order in question requires the payment of maintenance in a
-- lump sum -- 6 months." (2): "each month's worth of maintenance arrears" is "the total amount of maintenance that is
-- required to be paid by the respondent in a month under the maintenance order".
-- "(or any part thereof)" is read as rounding UP: $1,900 of arrears on a $600 order is 3 months' worth and a part, so 4.
-- Illustration to s 91P: arrears of $5,000 on a $1,000 order; the court imposed 2 months (within a 5-month cap).
GIVEN `the arrears` IS A NUMBER, `the monthly sum` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `months' worth of arrears, counting a part as one` IS
    IF `the arrears` <= 0 OR `the monthly sum` <= 0
    THEN 0
    ELSE 1 + `months' worth of arrears, counting a part as one` (`the arrears` - `the monthly sum`) `the monthly sum`

GIVEN `arrears at the application` IS A NUMBER, `the monthly sum` IS A NUMBER, `a lump-sum order` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the maximum imprisonment for arrears in months` IS
    IF `a lump-sum order`
    THEN 6
    ELSE `months' worth of arrears, counting a part as one` `arrears at the application` `the monthly sum`

-- s 81(5): the default term specified under s 81(2)(d) "must not exceed the following: (a) for each failure to prove payment
-- of any maintenance arrears ... -- 6 months; (b) for each failure to prove payment of any payable maintenance ... -- one month
-- for each month's worth of payable maintenance (or any part thereof)."
-- s 91O(1): the court may then sentence if the respondent fails to prove payment by the date AND "has shown no good cause";
-- (2): not beyond the term specified; (5): the sentence "does not affect or diminish the obligation" to pay.
GIVEN `failures to prove arrears paid` IS A NUMBER, `unproved payable maintenance` IS A NUMBER, `the monthly sum` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the maximum default term in months` IS
        6 * `failures to prove arrears paid`
    +   `months' worth of arrears, counting a part as one` `unproved payable maintenance` `the monthly sum`

GIVEN `failed to prove payment by the date` IS A BOOLEAN, `showed good cause` IS A BOOLEAN, `a term was specified` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may imprison for breaching the show-payment order` IF
    `a term was specified` AND `failed to prove payment by the date` AND (NOT `showed good cause`)

§§ `91L, 91N -- the banker's guarantee and community service`

-- s 91L(2): the banker's guarantee "(a) must be valid for a period specified by the court (not exceeding 3 years) ...; and
-- (b) must be for an amount specified by the court not exceeding 3 months of maintenance payable under the maintenance
-- order." (3): the original must be given to the claimant "within one month from the date of the order".
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the longest banker's guarantee in months` MEANS 36

GIVEN `the monthly sum` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the largest banker's guarantee` `the monthly sum` MEANS 3 * `the monthly sum`

-- s 91N(1): "A community service order mentioned in section 91B(1)(f) must not exceed 40 hours."
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the most community service hours` MEANS 40

§§ `71, 91C, 91D, 91K -- attachment of earnings`

-- s 71(1): "A court may, when making a maintenance order under section 69 or 70 or at any time thereafter, if the court
-- considers just, make an attachment of earnings order under this section to secure payment of any maintenance that will
-- become payable under the maintenance order." (3): no application under (1) or (2) "may be made if proceedings under Part 9
-- for the enforcement of the same maintenance order are pending."
-- So there are two routes to the same order: s 71 (securing FUTURE maintenance, outside enforcement) and s 91B(1)(g) (as an
-- enforcement order once the court is satisfied of breach). While a Part 9 application is pending, only the second is open.
GIVEN `the court considers it just` IS A BOOLEAN, `Part 9 enforcement proceedings are pending` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a section 71 attachment of earnings application may be made` IF
    `the court considers it just` AND (NOT `Part 9 enforcement proceedings are pending`)

-- s 91C: the amount is "a sum that seems reasonable to the court, after taking into account the resources and needs of the
-- respondent and the needs of persons for whom the respondent must or reasonably should provide". No figure, no cap.
-- s 91D(2): the order "need not be complied with until the expiry of 7 days after the date on which a copy ... is served".
-- s 91I: sums received discharge first the oldest sums due under the maintenance order, then costs.
-- INFERENCE: "until the expiry of 7 days" is read as: on day 8 counting service as day 0, compliance is due.
GIVEN `days since service on the employer` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the employer must already comply` IF
    `days since service on the employer` > 7

-- s 91K(1): an employer (or anyone) who fails to comply with s 91E(1) or (4), s 91G or a s 91F order, or gives a false notice
-- or statement, is liable "to a fine not exceeding $2,000 or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding 12 months or to
-- both". (2): a defence to a s 91E(1) charge to prove the person "took all reasonable steps to comply".
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine for disobeying an attachment of earnings order` MEANS 2000

GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum imprisonment for disobeying an attachment of earnings order in months` MEANS 12

§§ `83 -- the arrears report to a credit bureau`

-- s 83(1): "If the court has declared under section 81(2)(a) the amount of maintenance arrears under a maintenance order,
-- and any of that amount remains in arrears, any of the following persons may lodge a report with a designated credit
-- bureau ...: (a) the person to whom or for whose benefit the maintenance is payable; (b) the caregiver of a person mentioned
-- in paragraph (a); (c) an authorised representative of a person mentioned in paragraph (a); (d) an MEO."
-- This is NOT a court order and not on the s 91B list: it is a step the payee (or an MEO) may take after the declaration.
DECLARE ReportLodger IS ONE OF
    `the payee or the beneficiary`
    `the beneficiary's caregiver`
    `an authorised representative`
    `an MEO`
    `someone other than these`

GIVEN `the lodger` IS A ReportLodger, `the arrears have been declared by the court` IS A BOOLEAN, `amount still in arrears` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `may lodge an arrears report with the credit bureau` IF
        `the arrears have been declared by the court`
    AND `amount still in arrears` > 0
    AND CONSIDER `the lodger`
        WHEN `someone other than these` THEN FALSE
        OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `91R -- the three-year limit on arrears`

-- s 91R(2): unsecured arrears "(a) are recoverable as a debt from the defaulter; (b) ... provable in the defaulter's
-- bankruptcy; and (c) ... a debt due from the defaulter's estate."
-- (4): "Maintenance arrears are not recoverable in any proceedings ... from the defaulter if the amount accrued due more than
-- 3 years before the commencement of the proceedings, unless -- (a) other proceedings to recover the amount from the
-- defaulter had previously been commenced within 3 years (or any longer period that the court in those proceedings may have
-- allowed) after the amount accrued due; or (b) the court, under special circumstances, otherwise allows."
-- It is a bar the court may lift "under special circumstances", not a requirement of leave before applying.
-- INFERENCE: counted in whole months; exactly 36 months is not "more than 3 years".
GIVEN `months between the amount falling due and the proceedings` IS A NUMBER,
      `earlier proceedings were begun in time` IS A BOOLEAN, `the court allows it in special circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the arrears are recoverable` IF
        `months between the amount falling due and the proceedings` <= 36
    OR  `earlier proceedings were begun in time`
    OR  `the court allows it in special circumstances`
